PNP (PEI-598, Flow layer slice): the refusal bit on sk_buff.

Every refusal PNP emits for a REJECT verdict (an RST or ICMP unreachable it
builds itself, net/pnp/refuse.c) carries this bit, and every PNP seat waves
such a packet through without judging it — the ratified law "PNP does not
judge its own refusals". Refusals are verdict machinery, not traffic: an
inbound REJECT's RST used to cross the egress seat and could be dropped
(and mis-attributed) by an outbound rule; an outbound REJECT's forged peer
answer is delivered back through the receive path and would otherwise be
judged as inbound traffic. The bit lives inside the `headers` group so
clones and copies keep it. Configured out with CONFIG_PEIOS_PNP.

diff --git a/include/linux/skbuff.h b/include/linux/skbuff.h
--- a/include/linux/skbuff.h
+++ b/include/linux/skbuff.h
@@ -1030,6 +1030,9 @@
 	__u8			csum_not_inet:1;
 #endif
 	__u8			unreadable:1;
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_PEIOS_PNP)
+	__u8			pnp_refusal:1;	/* a refusal PNP itself emitted */
+#endif
 #if defined(CONFIG_NET_SCHED) || defined(CONFIG_NET_XGRESS)
 	__u16			tc_index;	/* traffic control index */
 #endif
